WEBVTT

00:01.060 --> 00:03.417
Here is a simple circuit: a battery, a

00:03.417 --> 00:05.722
resistor, and a capacitor all connected in

00:05.722 --> 00:06.081
a loop.

00:06.388 --> 00:08.335
Right now the capacitor is completely

00:08.335 --> 00:08.643
empty.

00:09.590 --> 00:11.865
When we close the loop, charge begins to

00:11.865 --> 00:12.130
flow.

00:12.447 --> 00:14.458
At first the push is strong, so the

00:14.458 --> 00:16.522
voltage on the capacitor rises quickly.

00:16.839 --> 00:19.221
After one time constant, the capacitor has

00:19.221 --> 00:21.496
already reached sixty-three percent of the

00:21.496 --> 00:22.343
battery voltage.

00:23.280 --> 00:25.607
As the capacitor fills, the remaining

00:25.607 --> 00:28.050
voltage difference gets smaller, so the

00:28.050 --> 00:29.097
current dies away.

00:29.446 --> 00:31.657
The curve flattens and approaches the

00:31.657 --> 00:33.460
battery voltage asymptotically.

00:33.809 --> 00:36.310
The exact equation is V_C equals V_0 times

00:36.310 --> 00:38.928
one minus e to the minus t over tau, where

00:38.928 --> 00:41.371
the time constant tau is simply R times C.

00:43.363 --> 00:45.401
If we double the resistance, the time

00:45.401 --> 00:46.267
constant doubles.

00:46.573 --> 00:48.713
The same charging process now takes twice

00:48.713 --> 00:50.598
as long — the curve is stretched out.
